UNIT intersection
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG intersection(...arrays: any[]) -> any[]
DEPENDS-ON none   # pure; zero runtime deps; no seam (no clock/RNG/env/io/net)

# =====================================================================
# CONTRACT (lodash _.intersection, custom-build npm module 4.4.0)
# Default export: module.exports = intersection.  Variadic.
# Returns a NEW array of the values present in ALL given arguments,
# compared with SameValueZero, ordered/deduped by the FIRST argument.
# Only the FIRST arg's array-like-object-ness gates the whole thing;
# if the first arg is not an array-like OBJECT the result is [].
# =====================================================================

BEHAVIOR  # ordered pipeline; node numbers are referenced from SCOPE

  # ---- Helper definitions (reproduce EXACTLY) ----------------------

  # H1. SameValueZero equality (the equality used throughout):
  #     eq(a, b)  ==  (a === b) OR (a !== a AND b !== b)
  #     i.e. strict === , EXCEPT NaN equals NaN, and +0 === -0 (so
  #     -0 and +0 are the same key). No type coercion: 1 !== "1".

  # H2. isObjectLike(v)  ==  (v is truthy) AND (typeof v === "object")
  #     -> true for arrays, plain objects, arguments objects, boxed
  #        primitives, Date, RegExp, Map, Set, typed arrays, etc.
  #     -> false for null, undefined, and ALL primitives
  #        (number, string, boolean, bigint, symbol).

  # H3. isLength(v) == (typeof v === "number") AND v > -1 AND
  #                    (v % 1 === 0) AND v <= 9007199254740991
  #     (a valid array-like length: non-negative safe integer).

  # H4. isFunction(v): true iff Object.prototype.toString.call(v) is
  #     "[object Function]" or "[object GeneratorFunction]".

  # H5. isArrayLike(v) == (v != null) AND isLength(v.length)
  #                       AND NOT isFunction(v)

  # H6. isArrayLikeObject(v) == isObjectLike(v) AND isArrayLike(v)
  #     -> TRUE for arrays, arguments objects, typed arrays,
  #        and plain objects that carry a valid numeric .length
  #        (e.g. {length:2, 0:'a', 1:'b'}).
  #     -> FALSE for primitives (incl. primitive strings — a string
  #        has a .length but is NOT object-like), null, undefined,
  #        functions, and objects with no/invalid .length.

  # H7. castArrayLikeObject(v) == isArrayLikeObject(v) ? v : []
  #     NOTE: when v is NOT array-like-object this returns a FRESH []
  #     (a brand-new array, NOT the same reference as v).

  # H8. arrayIncludes(arr, value): linear scan of arr (treating a
  #     null/undefined arr as length 0) returning whether `value`
  #     occurs, by SameValueZero (=== , with NaN-matches-NaN handled
  #     by a value!==value branch). +0/-0 match each other.

  # ---- Pipeline ----------------------------------------------------

  # N1 (collect args). Gather all call arguments into an ordered list
  #     `arrays` of length = arguments.length. Zero args => arrays = [].
  #     (Implementation note: lodash uses baseRest; the observable
  #      effect is simply "rest-collect every positional argument".)

  # N2 (cast each arg). mapped = arrays.map(castArrayLikeObject)   [H7]
  #     => each element of `mapped` is either the SAME reference as the
  #        corresponding arg (if it was array-like-object) or a fresh [].

  # N3 (first-arg gate).
  #     if (mapped.length > 0) AND (mapped[0] === arrays[0])
  #          then result = baseIntersection(mapped)   # go to N4
  #          else return []                            # short-circuit
  #     Semantics: mapped[0] === arrays[0] is TRUE exactly when the
  #     first arg WAS array-like-object (H7 returned it unchanged).
  #     If the first arg was NOT array-like-object, mapped[0] is a fresh
  #     [] !== arrays[0], so the whole call returns [].
  #     Corollary: zero args => mapped.length === 0 => return [].
  #     Corollary: a single array-like-object arg => N4 with that one
  #     array (produces its deduped, order-preserved values).

  # N4 (baseIntersection over `mapped`). Let arrs = mapped (length >= 1,
  #     arrs[0] is the first array; every element is an array-like).
  #     Let:
  #        length    = arrs[0].length          # iterate over first
  #        maxLength = MIN over all i of arrs[i].length   # result cap
  #        result    = []
  #     (There is no iteratee and no comparator in _.intersection, so
  #      `computed` == `value` and membership uses SameValueZero [H8].)
  #
  #     Iterate index = 0,1,2,... while index < length AND
  #                                    result.length < maxLength:
  #        let value = arrs[0][index]    # reading a hole yields undefined
  #        # ZERO NORMALIZATION (no comparator branch):
  #        #   value = (value !== 0) ? value : 0
  #        #   -> if value is +0 or -0, force value = +0 (positive zero).
  #        #   -> all other values (incl. NaN, which is !== 0) unchanged.
  #        # DEDUP against already-accepted results:
  #        if SameValueZero-includes(result, value):  # already taken
  #             continue                                # skip duplicate
  #        # MEMBERSHIP in EVERY OTHER array:
  #        for othIndex = (arrs.length - 1) down to 1:
  #             if NOT SameValueZero-includes(arrs[othIndex], value):
  #                  continue outer    # value missing from some array; skip
  #        # value is in all arrays and not yet seen:
  #        result.push(value)
  #     return result
  #
  #     NOTE on the zero-normalization vs dedup interaction: because
  #     value is normalized to +0 BEFORE the dedup check and before the
  #     push, a first array of [-0, 0] yields a single +0 in result
  #     (the -0 is normalized to +0, then 0 is a SameValueZero dup).
  #     The emitted zero is ALWAYS +0, never -0.
  #
  #     NOTE on maxLength: the result can never be longer than the
  #     SHORTEST input array. Once result.length reaches maxLength the
  #     loop stops even if more of arrs[0] remains. (For genuine set
  #     intersection this cap is never the binding constraint beyond the
  #     natural fact that the intersection cannot exceed the smallest
  #     set, but it is part of the exact contract.)

  # ---- Equality / element semantics summary (SameValueZero) --------
  #  * NaN is a member: [NaN] intersect [NaN] => [NaN].
  #  * +0 and -0 are the SAME element; the emitted value is +0.
  #  * No coercion: 1 and "1" are distinct; true and 1 are distinct.
  #  * Objects/arrays/functions compared by REFERENCE identity
  #    (same object in two arrays => kept; structurally-equal but
  #    distinct references => not kept).
  #  * undefined is a normal element (incl. sparse-array holes read as
  #    undefined): [undefined] intersect [undefined] => [undefined].
  #  * null is a normal element: [null] intersect [null] => [null].

  # ---- Non-array-like ARGUMENTS (not elements) ---------------------
  #  * FIRST arg not array-like-object (primitive string/number/boolean/
  #    bigint/symbol, null, undefined, function, plain object w/o valid
  #    .length) => result []  (gate N3).
  #  * A LATER arg not array-like-object is cast to a fresh [] (H7), so
  #    the intersection with [] is EMPTY => result []. (Membership
  #    against an empty array fails for every value.)
  #  * An arguments object / typed array / {length:k, 0:..,1:..} IS
  #    array-like-object and is treated as its indexed elements.
  #  * A primitive string arg is NOT array-like-object (not object-like)
  #    => as first arg gives [], as later arg gives [] (cast to []).

  # ---- Purity ------------------------------------------------------
  #  No mutation of any input. Returns a fresh array. Deterministic;
  #  no clock/RNG/env/io/network seam.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Inputs are arg-arrays (each tuple is the full variadic argument list).
# Expected is the returned array, compared structurally. The harness's
# codec round-trips the in-scope element/arg kinds (see SCOPE).

SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire pipeline N1..N4
  and helpers H1..H8 above. All equality is SameValueZero. There is NO
  carried-data table for this unit (no S-box / charmap / locale table).

  Demonstrated in-scope value/arg kinds (self-consistent with genInputs):
    - Element kinds inside in-scope arrays: numbers (incl. NaN,
      +Infinity, -Infinity, +0, -0, negatives, MAX_SAFE_INTEGER),
      strings, booleans, null, undefined (and sparse-array holes which
      read as undefined), bigint, plain objects/arrays compared by
      reference, RegExp, Set, Map, Uint8Array elements.
    - Argument kinds (the variadic positions): arrays; array-like
      objects ({length,0,1,...}); primitive strings/numbers/booleans/
      bigint; null; undefined; plain non-array-like objects. These
      exercise the array-like filtering (gate N3 / cast H7).
    - Cardinalities: zero args, one arg, two+ args; empty arrays;
      large arrays (>=120 to exercise the equivalent SetCache fast path
      — behavior is identical to the small path; the SIR specifies the
      observable result, not the cache mechanism).

  CARRIED DATA: none.

  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by
  genInputs because the oracle codec cannot faithfully persist them):
    - Function elements: a function as an ELEMENT would be compared by
      reference identity, but functions do not survive the
      JSON.stringify(encode(args)) round-trip, so they are excluded from
      genInputs. (A function as an ARG is non-array-like => [] by N3;
      still excluded from emission for codec faithfulness.) Documented
      here so a blind re-emitter still reconstructs the reference-
      identity rule.
    - Symbol elements: Symbols are unique and do not round-trip the
      codec; excluded from genInputs. Reference/identity equality under
      SameValueZero would apply (a shared Symbol kept, distinct Symbols
      not), but it is OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for grading.
    - Boxed primitives as elements (new Number/String/Boolean): they ARE
      object-like and compared by reference identity, but do not
      round-trip; excluded from emission, behavior documented (reference
      identity, never equal to the unboxed primitive).

